pponent can easily attack (e.g., by playing C5).\n- Playing F6 flips E5. E5 is an internal piece, surrounded by other discs. Flipping an internal disc is strategically superior as it does not give the opponent new immediate attack points on the outside.\n\nReason: Move (F, 6) is the best choice because it adheres to the strategy of keeping pieces in the center and flipping internal discs. It restricts the opponent's mobility by not offering new frontier hooks, whereas (D, 6) would expose D5 to easy counter-play.\n\nAnswer: (F, 6)"}
